 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Demographic indices describe a population with numbers.\nThe crude birth rate (BR) is the number of live births in a year for every 1000 people:\nBR = (number of births ÷ total population) × 1000. It is written in ‰ (per thousand).\nExample: a town of 50 000 people had 2000 births in a year. BR = 2000 ÷ 50 000 × 1000 = 40 ‰.\nBirth rates above 30 ‰ are high (many African countries); below 15 ‰ are low (Europe).\nSteps: (1) divide the births by the population; (2) multiply by 1000; (3) write ‰.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "b": 1800,
   "p": 45000,
   "k": 1000
  },
  "problem": "A town of {p} people recorded {b} births last year. Calculate its birth rate.",
  "steps": [
   "Divide the births by the population: {b} ÷ {p} = {b / p}.",
   "Multiply by {k}: {b / p} × {k} = {b / p * k}."
  ],
  "answer": "BR = {b / p * k} ‰",
  "checks": [
   "b / p * k == 40"
  ]
 },
